## Overview

**Skill ID:** `TASK_DIARY_SKILL`
**Type:** Session Memory · Execution Cache · State Tracker
**Scope:** Single agent session (persists across message turns within the same conversation)
**Compatible Agents:** General-purpose task agents, DevOps agents, Codex PMF agents, CLI orchestration agents

`TASK_DIARY_SKILL` gives an AI agent a structured, in-session memory layer — referred to as the **Task Diary** — that records every meaningful action taken, every system state observed, and every outcome produced during a conversation session. The diary acts as a living cache of verified facts about the environment, eliminating the need to re-run commands whose results are already known and still valid.

---

## Why This Skill Exists

Without session memory, an agent operating across multiple turns suffers from several critical failure patterns:

| Failure Pattern | Description | Consequence |
|---|---|---|
| **Redundant Execution** | Re-running a command already executed this session | Wasted time, duplicate side effects (e.g., double installs) |
| **State Blindness** | Not knowing which steps have already succeeded | Re-attempting finished work, breaking idempotency |
| **Conflicting Actions** | Running a command that contradicts an already-known environment state | Environment corruption, version conflicts |
| **Error Amnesia** | Forgetting an error that already occurred and re-triggering it | Infinite loops, cascading failures |
| **Dependency Fog** | Re-resolving dependency trees already resolved | Slow execution, potential version drift between checks |

`TASK_DIARY_SKILL` eliminates all five patterns by mandating a **lookup-before-execute** discipline and a **write-after-execute** discipline enforced at the agent behavior level.

